[[:db/retract 17592186168267 :answer-field/choices 17592186168272] [:db/retract 17592186168267 :answer-field/choices 17592186168268] [:db/retract 17592186168267 :answer-field/choices 17592186168269] [:db/retract 17592186168267 :answer-field/choices 17592186168270] [:db/retract 17592186168267 :answer-field/choices 17592186168271] [:db/retract 17592186168249 :answer-field/choices 17592186168250] [:db/retract 17592186168249 :answer-field/choices 17592186168251] [:db/retract 17592186168249 :answer-field/choices 17592186168252] [:db/retract 17592186168249 :answer-field/choices 17592186168253] [:db/retract 17592186168249 :answer-field/choices 17592186168254] {:db/id 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0d9f5142-140d-5b22-b957-299741979b12" "question-43b66f9d-fca5-54ae-b724-0bc204fa830b" "question-ef7933ca-459a-5897-8c5b-5b08b877b68b" "question-2346f7c5-c1ac-509c-ac43-d1a23908e3df" "question-8aae46a7-7405-5484-9202-26c3555ea95d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171872 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $\\Gamma(z)$ for $\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0?$\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{t^{z}}{te^t} \\, \\textrm dt$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{e^t}{t^{1-z}} \\, \\textrm dt$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt, \\qquad \\textrm{Re}(z) > 0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The upper bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral II matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{t^{z}}{te^t} \\, \\textrm dt = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1}e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt = \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\dfrac{e^t}{t^{1-z}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-58e93e99-d92a-5ce1-a5e1-f2a7ff45e177" "question-cb5b08f1-b473-53d8-ae61-be733c058e2d" "question-8f3249d6-edac-5050-9528-d13b2bdbb793" "question-d0d87553-dab1-5046-8598-8f79d15fc0d7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171870 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\Gamma(7).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\Gamma(7) = 6! = 720.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bd01511b-f45a-5cd4-b173-d099f3266062" "question-6bbc8ec2-1cd6-5115-ab81-c2fbacd13746" "question-38540fee-f0e4-596e-9eff-9715fa0a19af" "question-0fdfcf02-67ae-5263-baa1-a6ba5d9b1b6d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171871 :question/problem "Calculate $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z), \\qquad \\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d9f5142-140d-5b22-b957-299741979b12" :question/id #uuid "0d9f5142-140d-5b22-b957-299741979b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-107888" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\n- Integral II matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc13ac3c-08d2-5ebd-90fb-f6bd2706df71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc13ac3c-08d2-5ebd-90fb-f6bd2706df71" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc13ac3c-08d2-5ebd-90fb-f6bd2706df71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1365d528-3559-5e8c-aab0-a5fb4cd1c0ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1365d528-3559-5e8c-aab0-a5fb4cd1c0ed" "answer-461bf02a-0b0d-5764-8758-d03d9e57079c" "answer-eb1e7480-42fe-5890-a5bb-345d5a5e89d6" "answer-a3251647-5a25-5014-86dc-0d1aa75cf482" "answer-939b0a23-be39-5e33-a7e0-ca57fb9a21db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1365d528-3559-5e8c-aab0-a5fb4cd1c0ed" :answer/id #uuid "1365d528-3559-5e8c-aab0-a5fb4cd1c0ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-461bf02a-0b0d-5764-8758-d03d9e57079c" :answer/id #uuid "461bf02a-0b0d-5764-8758-d03d9e57079c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb1e7480-42fe-5890-a5bb-345d5a5e89d6" :answer/id #uuid "eb1e7480-42fe-5890-a5bb-345d5a5e89d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3251647-5a25-5014-86dc-0d1aa75cf482" :answer/id #uuid "a3251647-5a25-5014-86dc-0d1aa75cf482" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-939b0a23-be39-5e33-a7e0-ca57fb9a21db" :answer/id #uuid "939b0a23-be39-5e33-a7e0-ca57fb9a21db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43b66f9d-fca5-54ae-b724-0bc204fa830b" :question/id #uuid "43b66f9d-fca5-54ae-b724-0bc204fa830b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107829" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-259c3d61-272b-5770-a437-91dfc25962d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-259c3d61-272b-5770-a437-91dfc25962d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "259c3d61-272b-5770-a437-91dfc25962d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc3851a5-2ba9-5c44-a866-bd435cfdc216" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2e73fb0-5e26-5b77-80d8-96d4ac256043" "answer-dc3851a5-2ba9-5c44-a866-bd435cfdc216" "answer-c892a373-7ca8-579b-9d31-e6a6a98294b8" "answer-d21e8d2c-6a2c-503f-b857-9d2fb05ebfc0" "answer-509a5a08-2a17-53d6-bfc0-859e810cfe28"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2e73fb0-5e26-5b77-80d8-96d4ac256043" :answer/id #uuid "c2e73fb0-5e26-5b77-80d8-96d4ac256043" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc3851a5-2ba9-5c44-a866-bd435cfdc216" :answer/id #uuid "dc3851a5-2ba9-5c44-a866-bd435cfdc216" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c892a373-7ca8-579b-9d31-e6a6a98294b8" :answer/id #uuid "c892a373-7ca8-579b-9d31-e6a6a98294b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d21e8d2c-6a2c-503f-b857-9d2fb05ebfc0" :answer/id #uuid "d21e8d2c-6a2c-503f-b857-9d2fb05ebfc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-509a5a08-2a17-53d6-bfc0-859e810cfe28" :answer/id #uuid "509a5a08-2a17-53d6-bfc0-859e810cfe28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef7933ca-459a-5897-8c5b-5b08b877b68b" :question/id #uuid "ef7933ca-459a-5897-8c5b-5b08b877b68b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107838" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{3z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. $3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition. We use a substitution $s={t}^{3},$ then $3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\text{d}s.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)+2}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}(3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{s}^{z-1}{e}^{-s}\\,\\text{d}s=Γ(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a6b14e4-15be-5db4-bf56-15f3f68a2ded"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a6b14e4-15be-5db4-bf56-15f3f68a2ded" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6b14e4-15be-5db4-bf56-15f3f68a2ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45349eef-8d3d-5379-9b8b-6f793b4003b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14bc919f-37b1-5bfd-9092-4f4fec1c9b34" "answer-45349eef-8d3d-5379-9b8b-6f793b4003b9" "answer-7c6629d0-7785-58c3-802f-ce326ee02b82" "answer-9db4a047-e2d4-57ce-acfe-2fdba69d4b5f" "answer-f33dd163-2dc5-5312-88c8-a108bb20e78c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14bc919f-37b1-5bfd-9092-4f4fec1c9b34" :answer/id #uuid "14bc919f-37b1-5bfd-9092-4f4fec1c9b34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45349eef-8d3d-5379-9b8b-6f793b4003b9" :answer/id #uuid "45349eef-8d3d-5379-9b8b-6f793b4003b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c6629d0-7785-58c3-802f-ce326ee02b82" :answer/id #uuid "7c6629d0-7785-58c3-802f-ce326ee02b82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9db4a047-e2d4-57ce-acfe-2fdba69d4b5f" :answer/id #uuid "9db4a047-e2d4-57ce-acfe-2fdba69d4b5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f33dd163-2dc5-5312-88c8-a108bb20e78c" :answer/id #uuid "f33dd163-2dc5-5312-88c8-a108bb20e78c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2346f7c5-c1ac-509c-ac43-d1a23908e3df" :question/id #uuid "2346f7c5-c1ac-509c-ac43-d1a23908e3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-107833" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{z-1}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition.  The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6d3a595-3c97-56e6-b4e9-37484ca30241"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6d3a595-3c97-56e6-b4e9-37484ca30241" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d3a59